The wavelength of a particle is given by the de Broglie wavelength equation: $\lambda = h/p = h/(mv)$, where $h$ is Planck's constant, $m$ is the mass, and $v$ is the velocity.

(a) Truck:
$m = 10000 \text{ kg}$, $v = 27.8 \text{ m/s}$
$\lambda = (6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}) / (10000 \text{ kg} \times 27.8 \text{ m/s}) = (6.626 \times 10^{-34}) / (2.78 \times 10^6) \approx 2.39 \times 10^{-40} \text{ m} = 2.39 \times 10^{-38} \text{ cm}$

(b) Flea:
$m = 50 \text{ mg} = 50 \times 10^{-3} \text{ kg} = 5 \times 10^{-2} \text{ kg}$, $v = 1 \text{ m/s}$
$\lambda = (6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}) / (5 \times 10^{-2} \text{ kg} \times 1 \text{ m/s}) = (6.626 \times 10^{-34}) / (5 \times 10^{-2}) \approx 1.325 \times 10^{-32} \text{ m} = 1.325 \times 10^{-29} \text{ cm}$

(c) Water molecule:
$m = 18 \text{ amu} = 18 \times 1.66 \times 10^{-27} \text{ kg} = 29.88 \times 10^{-27} \text{ kg} = 2.988 \times 10^{-26} \text{ kg}$, $v = 500 \text{ m/s}$
$\lambda = (6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}) / (2.988 \times 10^{-26} \text{ kg} \times 500 \text{ m/s}) = (6.626 \times 10^{-34}) / (1.494 \times 10^{-23}) \approx 4.43 \times 10^{-11} \text{ m} = 4.43 \times 10^{-9} \text{ cm}$

Therefore, the wavelengths are approximately 2.39 x 10^-38 cm, 1.33 x 10^-29 cm, and 4.43 x 10^-9 cm. Comparing this to the options, option E is closest.

