We want to find an integer x such that 63x ≡ 1 (mod 100).
This is equivalent to finding an integer x such that 63x - 1 is divisible by 100.
We can write this as 63x - 1 = 100k for some integer k. Then 63x = 100k + 1.
We want to find the multiplicative inverse of 63 modulo 100. We can use the extended Euclidean algorithm.
We want to solve $63x + 100y = 1$.
$100 = 1 \cdot 63 + 37$
$63 = 1 \cdot 37 + 26$
$37 = 1 \cdot 26 + 11$
$26 = 2 \cdot 11 + 4$
$11 = 2 \cdot 4 + 3$
$4 = 1 \cdot 3 + 1$
$1 = 4 - 1 \cdot 3$
$1 = 4 - 1 (11 - 2 \cdot 4) = 3 \cdot 4 - 1 \cdot 11$
$1 = 3 (26 - 2 \cdot 11) - 1 \cdot 11 = 3 \cdot 26 - 7 \cdot 11$
$1 = 3 \cdot 26 - 7 (37 - 1 \cdot 26) = 10 \cdot 26 - 7 \cdot 37$
$1 = 10 (63 - 1 \cdot 37) - 7 \cdot 37 = 10 \cdot 63 - 17 \cdot 37$
$1 = 10 \cdot 63 - 17 (100 - 1 \cdot 63) = 27 \cdot 63 - 17 \cdot 100$
So, $27 \cdot 63 - 17 \cdot 100 = 1$. Thus, $27 \cdot 63 \equiv 1 \pmod{100}$.
So, the inverse of 63 modulo 100 is 27. Thus, $x \equiv 27 \pmod{100}$.
